**Test filters.** For this step these gates run only the tests their filter selects (plus any inherited expected-red tests); put the tests this step adds or changes where a filter value finds them:
- `constructor-unit-tests`: 

**How to implement.** A kit's snapshot is an import origin of its own, and the decision table keeps the kit's copy unless the user adds it (001 §E 'Resolution on open (the §B decision table, applied to the snapshot): same id, version and hash → the database entry; missing locally → the snapshot read-only, with "Add to my library"; divergence → reported; the kit keeps its snapshot; name-only collision → side by side, with the explicit re-binding', 'nothing changes in the database unconfirmed'; .spec-md §3.7: a kit's snapshot always opens the dialog). In `OpticalConstructor.Domain/CatalogueImport.fs`: (1) `ImportOrigin` (steps 93, 102) gains `KitSnapshotOrigin of kit : KitName` (step 122), documented as the library snapshot of the named kit. `describe` answers the kit's name, so a collision row reads `Silicon — from <kit>` (`rowName`, step 102). (2) `ImportDecision.decide` keeps every rule for the other origins and the order in which it tells the rows apart. Under `KitSnapshotOrigin` it sets the actions so that applying a row's default writes nothing. `SameContentHash` stays `ReuseHeldEntry`. `EntryIdNotHeld` defaults to `SkipIncomingEntry` (the kit's copy, read-only), with `[ AddIncomingEntry ]` (Add to my library). `VersionNotHeld` defaults to `SkipIncomingEntry`, with `[ AddIncomingVersion KeepLatestVersion ]`; the latest never moves, because the move condition names `CatalogueImportOrigin` (step 102). `DivergentContent` defaults to `SkipIncomingEntry`, with `[ ForkIncomingEntry ]`. `NameCollision` defaults to `SkipIncomingEntry`, with `AddIncomingEntry` and then one `RebindToHeldEntry` per same-name entry, in its order. A category offers no re-binding, because a kit binds samples, materials and presets, and a material names its category by id. `mode` and `split` are unchanged. The doc of `decide` says so in one to three lines. (3) Any other complete match on `ImportOrigin` that FS0025 reports answers the kit as `describe` does. Tests: `ImportDecisionTests` (step 93) gains two facts, under `KitSnapshotOrigin (KitName "Optics kit")` and the class's envelopes. `a kit snapshot keeps the kit's copy by default and offers to add it to the library`: a new id is `EntryIdNotHeld`, defaulting to `SkipIncomingEntry` with `[ AddIncomingEntry ]`. The held version is `SameContentHash`, reusing it. Silicon's built-in version 2 is `VersionNotHeld`, defaulting to `SkipIncomingEntry` with `[ AddIncomingVersion KeepLatestVersion ]`. Silicon's version 1 with another description is `DivergentContent`, defaulting to `SkipIncomingEntry` with `[ ForkIncomingEntry ]`. The origin's `describe` is `Optics kit`. `a kit snapshot named like a held entry sits side by side, re-binding an entry but never a category`: a new material named `Silicon` against two held `Silicon` entries is `NameCollision`, defaulting to `SkipIncomingEntry`, with `AddIncomingEntry` and then one `RebindToHeldEntry` per held entry in order; its `rowName` is `Silicon — from Optics kit`. A new category named like a held category offers `[ AddIncomingEntry ]` alone. The class's other facts keep their assertions.

**Acceptance.** After the kit origin lands, `ImportDecision.decide` under `KitSnapshotOrigin` MUST default every row to keeping the kit's copy or reusing the held entry, and MUST offer adding, forking or re-binding as 001 §E says; constructor-unit-tests MUST pass, including `a kit snapshot keeps the kit's copy by default and offers to add it to the library` and `a kit snapshot named like a held entry sits side by side, re-binding an entry but never a category`, the count rising by 2, with every other `ImportDecisionTests` fact passing as written; and build, elevation and the eleven s16 invariants MUST pass.
